<BaseLayout title="Pusher &#x26; Diverter Mechanisms for Conveyors | PackagingConveyor.com" description="Conveyor pushers and diverters: pneumatic pushers, servo pushers, blade diverters, pop-up transfers, and ARB diverters for packaging line product routing." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-functions/pusher-diverter/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema, faqSchema]}>
<h1>Pusher &amp; Diverter Mechanisms</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Conveyor pushers and diverters route products off a main conveyor line onto spur conveyors, into lanes, or into reject bins. Pushers apply a lateral force perpendicular to the conveyor direction using pneumatic cylinders or servo actuators. Diverters redirect product at an angle using pivoting blades, pop-up wheels, or activated roller belts. Hytrol, Intralox, Dorner, and Intelligrated (Honeywell) manufacture pusher and diverter mechanisms integrated into conveyor systems for packaging, distribution, and end-of-line routing applications.</p>
<h2 id="diff-heading">Pushers vs. Diverters</h2>
<p><strong>Pushers</strong> move product sideways, perpendicular to the conveyor travel direction. A push plate or blade extends from one side of the conveyor, contacts the product, and pushes it across the belt to the opposite side where a spur conveyor, chute, or bin receives it. The product's forward motion on the conveyor combines with the lateral push force, creating a diagonal trajectory. Pushers are fast (50-200ms cycle), compact, and handle lightweight to medium products.</p>
<p><strong>Diverters</strong> redirect product at an angle without applying a perpendicular force. A pivoting blade, set of angled pop-up wheels, or activated roller belt surface changes the product's direction using the product's existing forward momentum. The product follows a smooth, angled path from the main line to the spur line. Diverters handle heavier products more gently than pushers because they redirect rather than shove.</p>
<p>The choice between pusher and diverter depends on product weight, fragility, line speed, and the number of divert destinations. For <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject applications</a>, pushers are standard because reject products do not require gentle handling. For routing products to multiple packing stations or lane destinations, diverters provide gentler handling and support more spur lines per mechanism.</p>
<h2 id="pusher-heading">Pusher Mechanisms</h2>
<h3>Pneumatic Pushers</h3>
<p>Pneumatic pushers use compressed air cylinders to extend and retract a push plate. The cylinder fires on a PLC signal, the push plate contacts the product, pushes it across the conveyor, and retracts. Cycle time (extend + retract) is 50-200ms depending on stroke length and air pressure. Stroke lengths range from 6 inches to 36 inches, the stroke must exceed the conveyor width plus the product width to clear the product completely off the belt.</p>
<p>Pneumatic pushers require compressed air supply (80-100 PSI typical) and a solenoid valve. Air consumption increases with cycle rate and stroke length. A high-speed reject application cycling 60 times per minute at 18-inch stroke consumes significant air volume. Flow control valves on the cylinder adjust push speed, too fast damages product, too slow misses the timing window.</p>
<p>Pneumatic pushers suit most <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject system</a> applications, lane diverting for cases and cartons, and product routing at moderate speeds (up to 150 FPM). They are the lowest-cost pusher option and the simplest to maintain. Replacement cylinders and seals are commodity items.</p>
<h3>Servo Pushers</h3>
<p>Servo pushers use an electric servo motor driving a linear actuator (ball screw, belt drive, or rack-and-pinion) to extend and retract the push plate. Servo control provides programmable stroke length, push speed, acceleration profile, and position accuracy. Cycle times as fast as 30-100ms are achievable, and the servo can adjust stroke length on the fly for different product sizes without mechanical changes.</p>
<p>Servo pushers cost 3-5 times more than pneumatic pushers but eliminate compressed air requirements and provide superior control for high-speed, high-precision applications. The programmable stroke length allows one pusher to handle multiple product sizes by selecting a recipe in the PLC, no manual adjustment between product changeovers.</p>
<p>Servo pushers are preferred for high-speed <a href="/conveyor-functions/slug-loading/">slug loading</a> applications where the pusher must position product precisely for case packer infeed, and for multi-product lines where changeover time matters. The servo drive also provides diagnostic data (motor current, position error) that aids predictive <a href="/resources/conveyor-maintenance/">maintenance</a>.</p>
<h2 id="diverter-heading">Diverter Mechanisms</h2>
<h3>Blade Diverters</h3>
<p>Blade diverters use a pivoting arm (blade) that swings into the product path at a 30 or 45 degree angle. The blade redirects product off the main conveyor onto a spur conveyor. When not diverting, the blade parks flush with the conveyor side rail, allowing product to pass straight. Blade diverters handle medium to heavy products (cases, totes, bundles) at speeds up to 300+ FPM.</p>
<p>The blade pivot point is upstream, and the blade tip reaches across the conveyor at the divert angle. Product contacts the blade face and slides along it onto the spur line. The blade surface is typically UHMW polyethylene or stainless steel, low friction to prevent product stalling against the blade. Hytrol's QUIK DIVERT and Intralox's ARB technology offer blade-style diverting integrated into roller and belt conveyor platforms.</p>
<p>Blade diverters require more conveyor length than pushers because the blade extends at an angle across the belt, a 45-degree blade on a 24-inch-wide conveyor extends 24 inches along the conveyor length. Product must clear the blade before the next product arrives. At high speeds with tight product spacing, the blade must cycle quickly or multiple consecutive products divert together.</p>
<h3>Pop-Up Wheel &amp; Roller Diverters</h3>
<p>Pop-up wheel diverters use rows of angled wheels or rollers that rise through the conveyor surface (between rollers or through belt slots) to redirect product. When retracted, wheels sit below the conveyor surface and product travels straight. When activated, wheels pop up above the surface, contact the product bottom, and steer it at the wheel angle (30 or 45 degrees) onto the spur line.</p>
<p>Pop-up diverters provide smooth, gentle diverting with no lateral impact force. They suit flat-bottom products, cases, cartons, trays, totes, that ride stably on the angled wheels. They cannot handle products with irregular bottoms or products too light to engage the wheel friction. <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">Transfer conveyors</a> use pop-up roller and wheel mechanisms for right-angle product routing.</p>
<h3>Activated Roller Belt (ARB)</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/activated-roller-belt/">Activated roller belt (ARB)</a> technology, pioneered by Intralox, embeds small angled rollers within a modular belt surface. When the belt rollers pass over an activation zone (a raised surface beneath the belt), the rollers engage and steer product at a 30-degree angle off the belt. Outside the activation zone, rollers are inactive and product travels straight.</p>
<p>ARB diverters operate at full conveyor speed with no mechanical cycling, the activation zone is always active. Product selection (divert vs. straight) is controlled by lane position. Products in the activation lane divert; products outside the activation lane pass straight. This allows very high throughput (100+ diverts per minute) with no speed reduction. ARB diverters handle lightweight to medium products (pouches, cartons, small cases) and excel in high-speed <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">laning applications</a>.</p>
<h3>Sliding Shoe Sorter</h3>
<p>Sliding shoe sorters use an array of small sliding shoes mounted on a slat conveyor surface. Individual shoes shift diagonally to push product off the side at specific divert points. Each shoe is independently controlled, allowing products to divert to different spur lines along the sorter length. Sliding shoe sorters handle the highest sort rates, 200+ diverts per minute, and route to 20 or more spur destinations.</p>
<p>Sliding shoe sorters are primarily used in distribution and fulfillment rather than packaging lines, but they appear in high-volume packaging operations that route finished cases to multiple palletizing stations or shipping lanes. Dematic, Intelligrated, and Beumer manufacture sliding shoe <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a>. See the <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems guide</a> for sortation system architecture.</p>
