<h2 id="selection-heading">Selecting a Pusher or Diverter</h2>
<p>Match the mechanism to these parameters:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Product weight and fragility.</strong> Lightweight, durable products (cartons, pouches) tolerate pusher impact. Heavy or fragile products (glass bottles, trays) need diverters that redirect without impact. Products over 25 kg require blade or pop-up diverters rather than pushers.</li>
<li><strong>Line speed and product spacing.</strong> Calculate the available time between products: Time (ms) = (Product gap in inches / Belt speed in inches per second) x 1000. The mechanism cycle time must be shorter than this window. At 200 FPM with 6-inch gaps, each window is 150ms, only pneumatic and servo pushers are fast enough.</li>
<li><strong>Number of divert destinations.</strong> Single-destination diverting uses pushers or blade diverters. Multi-destination routing (3+ spurs) uses sliding shoe sorters or sequential blade diverters. ARB technology handles 2-3 angled divert lanes.</li>
<li><strong>Compressed air availability.</strong> Pneumatic pushers and some blade diverters require compressed air. Servo pushers, ARB, and pop-up wheels use electric power only. Facilities without compressed air infrastructure should specify electric mechanisms.</li>
<li><strong>Changeover frequency.</strong> Lines running multiple product sizes benefit from servo pushers with programmable stroke length. Pneumatic pushers require manual stroke adjustment between product sizes. Blade diverters and ARB mechanisms handle multiple product sizes without adjustment.</li>
</ol>
